  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 AM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nScattered thunderstorms continue to move across the area early\nthis morning. There have been scattered reports of strong\nwinds/wind damage or large hail with the storms across east- \ncentral WI. Meanwhile, still watching a line of thunderstorms \nentering northwest WI as of 2:30 am. Still some uncertainty\non how far east these storms will make it into northeast WI\nlater this morning. Otherwise, storms should come to an end\nby mid-morning. \n\nOtherwise, skies will become partly cloudy with warm and humid\nconditions this afternoon. The combination of the heat and\nhumidity is expected to trigger isolated thunderstorms late\nthis afternoon into early this evening. Still plenty of\ninstability and shear that may cause some of the storms to\nbecome strong or severe with damaging winds and large hail. \n\nDry conditions are expected this weekend, then the chances of\nthunderstorms arrive late Monday and continue into Tuesday\nnight as a cold front moves across the area. Stronger storms\nare possible Tuesday and Tuesday night. Above normal temperatures\nwill continue into the middle of next week with highs in the\nmiddle 70s to middle 80s. Dry conditions expected Wednesday\nand Thursday as high pressure moves across the region.",
